Build dependencies
PlatformIO Core 6.2.0 is installed separately under its Apache-2.0 license.
https://github.com/platformio/platformio-core/tree/v6.2.0
The pinned Tasmota source selects its ESP8266 platform, Arduino framework and
toolchain. Their own licenses and notices remain in those dependencies. Python,
GCC, patch and optional sanitizer runtimes are separately installed host tools.

ESP8266 framework and platform sources
dependencies.lock.json pins the exact Tasmota ESP8266 platform 2026.04.00 and
Arduino framework 2.7.8 source/package archives by size and SHA-256. The build uses
those verified archives and includes them unchanged with firmware. The framework
retains its LGPL-2.1 license and component notices, including eboot (BSD-3-Clause),
BearSSL (MIT), libb64, umm_malloc, LittleFS, SdFat and the Espressif SDK license.
Some SDK components are supplied upstream as binary libraries under their own
ESP8266-specific license; they are not represented as project-authored source.
The SDK also supplies prebuilt C/math libraries; these are distinct from the
compiler executable and retain their upstream package conditions. The framework's
lwIP glue, options, patches and BSD notice are paired with the exact official
lwIP 2.1.2 source ZIP recorded in dependencies.lock.json.
Full notices stay at their original paths inside the framework archive. The
platform build scripts retain their Apache-2.0 notice. See
docs/dependencies.md for provenance, distribution and reproducibility boundaries.
The project's tools/patches/platform-espressif8266-2026.04.00.patch modifies
platform.py and builder/penv_setup.py on 2026-10-15 to keep Core in charge of SCons
and reuse the constrained host Python environment. Both modified files carry
dated notices and retain their Apache headers. The derived platform.json version
and description identify that dated patch variant. The original source ZIP stays
unchanged in the distribution; the matching project archive supplies the patch
and derivation recipe.
The linked GCC target runtimes carry GPL terms with the GCC Runtime Library
Exception 3.1; the original GCC 4.8.2 exception text is preserved at
licenses/GCC-RUNTIME-EXCEPTION-3.1.txt in the matching project source archive.

Corresponding source
tools/build.py preserves the source-only project archive, exact Tasmota archive,
and exact ESP8266 framework/platform and lwIP source archives alongside firmware.
Together they preserve editable modifications, upstream sources, build scripts,
dependency pins and original third-party notices. Distribute all five archives,
their manifests, LICENSE and NOTICE with binary releases. A link to this repository
alone is not a substitute for supplying the matching source bundle. The GPL's
Corresponding Source requirement is described in LICENSE sections 1 and 6.

